# contingent: S2 grounds "possible not to be" in generation and corruption, and S3 reads it as "at some time is not". Both are the same corruptibility, so the sense does not shift.
# The known weakness of S4 (each thing fails to exist at some time, so at some one time nothing exists) is a quantifier-scope move, not a change in what "exists" means.
# necessary: S7 means "not able not to be". S8-S10 then divide necessary beings by whether their necessity is caused by another. I read this as subdividing one sense, not a second sense.
